  "course_key": "g3-sec1-math",
  "unit_key": "g3-sec1-g5",
  "position": 2,
  "status": "draft",
  "title": "Problems involving perimeter and area of composite plane figures",
  "summary": "Decompose plane figures, subtract cut-outs and account for shared edges.",
  "outcomes": [
    "5.2"
  ],
  "prerequisite_lessons": [
    "g5-lesson-01"
  ],
  "estimated_minutes": 55,
  "objectives": [
    "Split a composite figure into familiar plane shapes.",
    "Use subtraction for holes and cut-outs.",
    "Find outside perimeter without counting internal shared edges."
  ],
  "mastery_policy_key": "g5-default-v1",
  "sections": [
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-52-method",
      "position": 1,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Core method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Decompose plane figures, subtract cut-outs and account for shared edges. Keep every measurement in compatible units before substituting."
        },
        {
          "type": "display_math",
          "latex": "A_{composite}=\\sum A_{parts}-\\sum A_{cutouts}"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-02-visual",
      "position": 2,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Read the diagram",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The diagram shows the L-shape in the second worked example: a 15 m by 9 m rectangle with a 5 m by 3 m corner removed. Its area can be found by subtracting the corner from the whole rectangle, or by splitting the L-shape into two rectangles that do not overlap. Its perimeter is the length of the outside boundary only; a line drawn inside the shape to split it is not part of the perimeter."
        },
        {
          "type": "asset_ref",
          "asset_key": "g5-lesson-02-diagram"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-02-reasoning",
      "position": 3,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Reasoning method",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Mark every required length before calculating. Missing boundary lengths often come from subtraction along a common total width or height. For area, choose a decomposition that avoids overlap. For perimeter, trace the boundary once in order and list each exposed segment."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-52-example",
      "position": 4,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: subtract a cut-out",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 12 cm by 8 cm rectangle has a 4 cm by 3 cm corner removed. Find the remaining area."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Find the area of the whole rectangle and of the removed corner."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "12\\times8=96\\text{ cm}^2,\\qquad4\\times3=12\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Subtract the corner, because it is no longer part of the shape."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A=96-12=84\\text{ cm}^2"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "Check by splitting instead: a 12 cm by 5 cm strip and an 8 cm by 3 cm strip give 60 + 24 = 84 cm²."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-02-example-2",
      "position": 5,
      "type": "worked_example",
      "title": "Worked example: area and outside perimeter",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 15 m by 9 m rectangle has a 5 m by 3 m corner removed, as in the diagram. Find the remaining area and the outside perimeter."
        }
      ],
      "steps": [
        {
          "position": 1,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Area: subtract the removed corner from the whole rectangle."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "A=15\\times9-5\\times3=135-15=120\\text{ m}^2"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 2,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Perimeter: trace the outside edges once. The top edge is 15 − 5 = 10 m and the right edge is 9 − 3 = 6 m."
            },
            {
              "type": "display_math",
              "latex": "P=15+9+10+3+5+6=48\\text{ m}"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "position": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "The two edges of the cut, 5 m and 3 m, replace equal lengths of the original top and right sides, so the perimeter is the same as the full rectangle's: 2(15 + 9) = 48 m."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "verification_note": "The area 120 m² is less than the full rectangle's 135 m², and the boundary trace uses each of the six outside edges once."
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-02-pitfalls",
      "position": 6,
      "type": "explanation",
      "title": "Common errors and checks",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Do not add the area of a cut-out. Do not count shared edges in the outside perimeter. Area and perimeter use different units and cannot be combined in the same arithmetic expression."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-52-recall",
      "position": 7,
      "type": "active_recall",
      "title": "Check your understanding",
      "prompt": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "A 10 cm by 6 cm rectangle has a 2 cm by 3 cm corner removed. Find the remaining area in cm²."
        }
      ],
      "response": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "comparison_mode": "exact_numeric",
        "canonical_answer": "54",
        "canonical_latex": "54",
        "accepted_answers": [],
        "absolute_tolerance": null,
        "rounding_precision": null,
        "unit": null
      },
      "feedback": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Subtract the cut-out: 60 - 6 = 54."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "stable_key": "g5-lesson-02-summary",
      "position": 8,
      "type": "summary",
      "title": "Method checklist",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Area of a composite figure: add the areas of parts that do not overlap, or subtract cut-outs from a larger shape."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Perimeter: trace the outside boundary once, find a missing edge by subtracting along a full width or height, and never count an internal dividing line. Area is in square units; perimeter is in length units."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
